I realize that the thread this belongs in is about two weeks dead by now -
but I wanted to say that even though NetHack *does* calculate phases of the
moon, that's not what's relevant for finding Easter (as that calculation
depends upon an imaginary Paschal Moon), and thus implementing *good*
code for days when the Easter Bunny leaves a trail of (dragon?) eggs
would take a bit more adding-on than some people indicated (dev team,
are you listening?). Reposted from sci.math/rec.puzzles, with a spoiler for
the truly uninterested:



cet1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Chris Thompson) writes:
>spurdy@pomona.claremont.edu writes:
>|> 	I've forgotten.  In general practice, what is the rule for Easter?
>
>"General practice" in the West is to follow the Gregorian algorithm. The
>Eastern Orthodox churches do not do this, of course.
>
>One of the best collections of practically useful calendrical algorithms
>is to be found in the second volume of Winning Ways (Berlekamp, Conway, Guy).
>Here is a brief summary for calculating Easter:
>
>Easter is on the first Sunday *strictly* after the Paschal Full Moon. The PFM
>is on
>
>   March 50 - (11g - c) rem 30  =  April 19 - (11g - c) rem 30
>
>where g = 1 + (year rem 19) [the "Golden number"] and c is calculated as
>indicated below;
>
>EXCEPT that if this gives April 19, replace it by April 18; and if it gives
>       April 18 and g >= 12, then replace it by April 17.
>
>c is caclulated by  
>
>   c = C - C div 4 - (8*C+13) div 25 - 3    where C = year div 100
>
>and is equal to 6 for 1900 <= year <= 2199.
>
>Throughout the above (A div B) means floor (A/B) and (A rem B) means A-B*(A div B).
>
>|> Also, Good Friday this year was on April Fool's Day, which I found somewhat
>|> amusing.  When, if at all, can we expect Easter to be on April Fool's Day?
>
>It last happened in 1956, and will next happen in 2018. This is an unusally
>long gap. It is really quite easy to work these things out. If you want an
>example to work on: find when Easter has occurred or will occur on (a) March 22
>& (b) April 25, and explain the long-term frequencies, and why the latter is
>more common.
>
>Followup set to rec.puzzles only. This may be fun, but it isn't mathematics.

